  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Connecticut has $15.4B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. Obligations fell 56.0% year over year. Those obligations came through 20,979 awards with an average award size of $736K. The Department of Defense accounted for most of the spending at $15.1B. Other buying agencies each accounted for much smaller totals, led by the Department of Veterans Affairs at $107M and the Department of Homeland Security at $105M. Top vendors in the state are concentrated in defense and aerospace, led by ELECTRIC BOAT CORPORATION at $8.5B, RTX CORPORATION at $3.7B, and SIKORSKY AIRCRAFT CORPORATION at $2.4B. Industry dollars are similarly weighted: Engineering Services received $6.8B, Aircraft Manufacturing received $2.9B, and Ship Building and Repairing received $1.7B. Fiscal-year totals over the last six years show variability: FY21 was $18.4B, FY22 was $22.3B, FY23 was $26B, FY24 was $20B, FY25 was $35.1B, and FY26 is $15.4B. All figures are contract obligations; current-year totals are partial (fiscal year to date).",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "What changed in Connecticut federal spending this year?",
        "body": "Obligations fell 56.0% year over year in FY2026 to date. Total obligations for FY26 are $15.4B, down from FY25 at $35.1B. The drop is visible across annual totals: FY24 was $20B and FY23 was $26B. Changes in agency and vendor concentration accompanied the decline."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Where does federal contract money go within Connecticut?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is the dominant buyer at $15.1B in FY2026 to date. Top vendors are ELECTRIC BOAT CORPORATION at $8.5B, RTX CORPORATION at $3.7B, and SIKORSKY AIRCRAFT CORPORATION at $2.4B. Major industry recipients include Engineering Services at $6.8B, Aircraft Manufacturing at $2.9B, and Ship Building and Repairing at $1.7B."
